[h1]Flavorium Universalis - Rhomania v0.4.0: EU5 1.4[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.4.* | Requires: Flavorium Universalis and the Fate of the Phoenix content pack[/i]

[hr][/hr]

Updated for EU5 1.4. The version jumps to 0.4 to match the game version. Not compatible with 1.3.

[h2]New: The Komnenian Question[/h2]

A chain over about ten years that settles the dispute with Trebizond over the Roman title.

[list]
[*] [b]Opening:[/b] the Komnenian Question decision (25 political influence; needs Constantinople, positive stability, 50 legitimacy, no regency and one opening condition), or by itself when the two courts share a dynasty, are allied, are hostile, or Trebizond is in trouble. Each condition that holds improves the starting position.
[*] [b]Accord and Pretext:[/b] two scores built over five stages (the Pontic court, the Turkmen frontier, the Despot title, rival suitors). Option tooltips show how each choice moves them.
[*] [b]Union:[/b] proposing a union rolls, with the odds shown, between acclamation (Trebizond is annexed), a personal union with Byzantium as senior partner, and refusal. Higher Accord shifts the odds toward acclamation. A human Trebizond chooses whether to accept.
[*] [b]Reunify the Pontus:[/b] a casus belli against Trebizond, granted by declaring its crown a usurpation or as the fallback after a refusal. It allows annexation or a vassal Despotate of the Pontus at the peace table, both cheaper with more Pretext.
[*] [b]No Second Emperor:[/b] the mission task now also completes when Trebizond is a non-tributary subject or our junior union partner.
[/list]

[h2]New: The Latin Empress[/h2]

Completing [b]A Latin Bride[/b] opens a chain of about ten years with the Catholic great power married into the dynasty.

[list]
[*] [b]A Latin Bride:[/b] now needs our ruler or heir married to the ruler or heir of a Catholic great power that is not at war with us. Any royal marriage with a Catholic court used to count.
[*] [b]Two branches:[/b] if their princess came to Constantinople, decide whether she takes an Orthodox name or keeps a Latin chapel, and whether to hire the company of knights her family sends. Their pay comes due five to eight years later. If our princess went west, decide whether she joins the Roman Church and what she takes with her: scholars, the palace ceremonial, or letters home read as intelligence.
[*] [b]Shared stages:[/b] the in-laws' merchants ask for a quarter and customs exemptions, the Pope writes about church union (the letter reads where the union arc stands), and the in-laws propose a crusade against the strongest Muslim neighbour. The crusade waits while either court is at war.
[*] [b]Settlement:[/b] choices build a bond score, and the settlement ten years on is tiered by it.
[*] [b]Closing early:[/b] the chain ends if the partner stops being a Catholic great power, goes to war with us, or the couple die, separate, or leave the line of succession. A union of crowns is left to the normal succession rules.
[/list]

[h2]New: The Serenissima Question[/h2]

Completing [b]The Venetian Alliance[/b] (which now also needs Constantinople and a Venice that is no one's subject and at peace with us) opens ten stages over about fifty years.

[list]
[*] [b]Stages:[/b] renewing or revoking the 1082 customs exemption, Venetian loans and the crown jewels if they are in pawn, the Genoese war, a sea league against the Turks, Hungary pressing on Dalmatia, the Venetian mainland (where an Italian exarch has a say), buying or swapping a Venetian port in the Morea or the Aegean, the doge's old ducal title against a vicariate from the western emperor, and a gift to St Mark's. Genoa is the alternative partner throughout.
[*] [b]Concord and Leverage:[/b] two scores set the ending. Diplomat, admiral and merchant ministers open extra options.
[*] [b]Endings:[/b] Venice as the [b]Ducate of Venetia[/b], a new subject that keeps its republic, fleet and colonies, pays a small tribute, lets our merchants trade free of its tariffs and can never be annexed; a perpetual alliance; a friendship; or a rupture with a 25-year war claim on the Stato da Mar ports. The Ducate and the alliance can also keep Venice out of the Holy Roman Empire.
[*] [b]Human Venice:[/b] a player controlling Venice decides the port deal and the final proposal.
[*] [b]Closing early:[/b] if Venice is gone or someone's subject, we lose Constantinople, or a war with Venice lasts ten years.
[/list]

[h2]New: The Academy[/h2]

[b]The Academy Reopened[/b] now needs five universities and Constantinople or Athens, and opens a chain of six stages over six to ten years.

[list]
[*] [b]Stages:[/b] who leads the Academy (the Latin scholastics, the Palamite monks or Plethon's circle), which faculties open, what the library translates, how to answer the Patriarch, and a public disputation where one of your Court Ledger schools can compete. Scholar, jurist, strategist and theologian ministers open extra options.
[*] [b]The charter:[/b] one reward, plus one if the Church was reconciled and one if your school won the disputation. Rewards: an [b]Imperial Academy[/b] policy for Elite Education; +4 maximum literacy for nobles and burghers at every university you own (including ones built later); faster institution growth at every university; military tactics scaling with your universities, up to +0.2 at twenty; a law faculty; and a theology faculty.
[*] [b]Latinization and Hellenization:[/b] the choices move the slider, and the winning party adds a twenty-year drift. The old-rites thread now opens only when Plethon's circle wins the Academy, not on completing the task.
[/list]

[h2]The West[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]New panel:[/b] the two counters with how far each moved in the last year, a map of the four endings with their thresholds, one card per prefecture (exarch, loyalty, appetite, whether his office is hereditary, whether he is disaffected, whether a cession review is due) with that province's actions, and a Consistory tab with the vote count. The situation now has its own icon.
[*] [b]Per-prefecture actions:[/b] paying one province's garrisons, conferring a court title (new: prestige for loyalty), confirming an exarch's son, sending a strategos, summoning an exarch and chartering a commune now act on one prefecture, each with its own cooldown. The census, the full donative and the tribute still act on the whole west.
[*] [b]Appetite per exarch:[/b] each exarch has his own appetite for more of his province. It was a single value for the whole west.
[*] [b]Cession review:[/b] every five years, land we hold in a prefecture's region is offered to its exarchate, as the terms of appointment require.
[*] [b]When an exarch dies:[/b] confirm his heir, which makes the office hereditary, or send one of your two ablest loyal courtiers. Whether the province accepts the new man depends on his ability, imperial grip, western autonomy, the old exarch's loyalty and whether the office was hereditary. A sitting consistory can also name the successor.
[*] [b]Exarchates[/b] gain +30 loyalty to their overlord.
[/list]

[h2]Mission tree[/h2]

The conquest missions no longer wait for The State Restored, which could take a century and let the player outrun the tree. [b]The Anatolian Return[/b] now opens off The Walls and [b]The Balkan Frontier[/b] off Thessaloniki, so the Ottoman fork, the Themes, the Bulgarian and Serbian questions, Danube Reached, the Adriatic and No Second Emperor are available alongside the three pillars. Anatolia Secured and the Levant after it, and Illyricum and the western reconquest, still need The State Restored.

Literacy requirements now read the estate that does the work, not the realm average, which low peasant literacy drags down as the empire expands:
[list]
[*] [b]A Literate Administration:[/b] nobles 40% and burghers 25% (was 25% realm average). The matching auto-modifier starts at the same thresholds and grows stronger above them.
[*] [b]The Academy:[/b] nobles 35%, now as the requirement for the Academy's literacy reward (see The Academy above).
[*] [b]Greek Fire:[/b] burghers 45%, in the mission and in the action. In the Greek Fire event, the monastic archives route needs clergy 35%.
[*] [b]The Notitia:[/b] nobles 50%.
[/list]

[b]Embassies to the West[/b] now needs three great powers in western Europe with an opinion of us of 150 or more, instead of four diplomats.

[h2]Political influence[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Appoint an Exarch:[/b] 20 political influence and 10 government power. It was free.
[*] [b]Confirm an Exarch's Son, Charter a Commune, Send a Strategos (each per prefecture), Elevate a Senior Exarch and Convene the Consistory:[/b] 5 political influence each. They were free.
[/list]

[h2]Advances[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Exarchal Chancery[/b] no longer requires Renovatio Imperii, because 1.4 does not allow an advance to require one from an earlier age. It still needs Latinization above 25 and Constantinople as the capital, which are Renovatio's own conditions, so the same emperors qualify.
[/list]

[h2]Bureaucracies[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Bureau Steward:[/b] the main mod's steward now reduces the stewarded bureau's malus by 25% or more, scaled by the minister. This covers the eleven Byzantine bureaucracies as well.
[/list]

[h2]Fixes[/h2]

[list]
[*] The West and Mamluk situation panels no longer log layout errors.
[*] [b]An Office That Works fired too often.[/b] A well-run empire failed every other bureaucracy event's conditions, so this one took the whole yearly roll. It now has its own fifteen-year cooldown and half the weight.
[*] [b]Missing diplomacy text:[/b] the Exarchate's relation line and cancel option, and the offer to make a country an Exarchate.
[/list]
